    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten",
    "problem": "Given any two points in a plane, there is a unique line that passes through both points. Given six points in a plane, what is the maximum number of unique  lines that can be made using these points?",
    "worked_solution": "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ points, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those points for each of the lines, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of unique lines is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
